{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/b1-explainer-about-labour-productivity-gdp-per-hour-ireland-norway-and-luxembourg-teach-wh/audio/main-ff6687949f91.mp3","durationSec":254.04,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":4,"text":"Labour productivity compares economic output with the hours people work."},{"start":4.66,"end":7.5,"text":"A common measure is GDP per hour worked."},{"start":8.16,"end":10.82,"text":"GDP means the total value produced in an economy."},{"start":11.56,"end":15.46,"text":"Imagine that a bakery produces goods worth 800 dollars during 10 hours of work."},{"start":15.94,"end":17.96,"text":"Its output is 80 dollars per hour."},{"start":18.58,"end":22.06,"text":"However, this measure is not simply a score for personal effort."},{"start":22.06,"end":28.58,"text":"Workers may produce more because they use better machines, faster technology, or a more effective system."},{"start":29.36,"end":31.92,"text":"Investment and workplace organisation also matter."},{"start":32.56,"end":37.76,"text":"A person using modern equipment may produce more than an equally hardworking person using old equipment."},{"start":38.62,"end":43.06,"text":"Therefore, high GDP per hour does not automatically mean that people are working harder."},{"start":44.04,"end":48.12,"text":"To compare countries fairly, researchers use purchasing power parities."},{"start":48.12,"end":50.7,"text":"These adjust for differences in prices between countries."},{"start":51.3,"end":56.86,"text":"Without this adjustment, the same amount of money could represent very different quantities of goods and services."},{"start":57.48,"end":61.8,"text":"In the comparison used here, Ireland ranks first for GDP per hour."},{"start":62.8,"end":65.34,"text":"Norway and Luxembourg are also among the top three."},{"start":66.48,"end":73.98,"text":"In 2023, the average across OECD countries was around 70 US dollars of GDP per hour."},{"start":74.58,"end":77.96,"text":"The strongest economies produced nearly twice that average."},{"start":78.92,"end":83.12,"text":"This large gap is interesting, but the ranking needs careful interpretation."},{"start":83.96,"end":88.96,"text":"A country’s industrial structure can strongly affect its result."},{"start":90.1,"end":95.62,"text":"The number does not tell us everything about ordinary workers, wages, or daily living conditions."},{"start":96.72,"end":102.18,"text":"Ireland is home to many foreign-owned multinational enterprises, especially in technology and pharmaceuticals."},{"start":103.36,"end":108.62,"text":"These companies can report very large profits and valuable intellectual property in Ireland."},{"start":109.12,"end":112.2,"text":"Aircraft leasing also adds to measured economic activity."},{"start":112.98,"end":117.28,"text":"Together, these global activities can make Irish GDP extremely large."},{"start":118.06,"end":122.4,"text":"However, much of this value may have a limited connection with local jobs and production."},{"start":123.26,"end":130.42,"text":"If GDP is very high while the number of working hours is relatively small, GDP per hour becomes very high too."},{"start":131.04,"end":133.7,"text":"This helps explain Ireland’s first-place position."},{"start":134.74,"end":141.92,"text":"It does not mean that every employee in Ireland produces more through personal skill or effort than every employee elsewhere."},{"start":142.76,"end":148.26,"text":"The result partly reflects how multinational business activity appears in Ireland’s national accounts."},{"start":149.16,"end":154.74,"text":"Ireland, Norway, and Luxembourg all have high GDP per hour, but their economies are not identical."},{"start":155.5,"end":160.6,"text":"Ireland’s figure is strongly affected by multinational technology and pharmaceutical companies."},{"start":160.98,"end":164.84,"text":"Norway has a large energy sector, which creates high-value output."},{"start":165.8,"end":170.5,"text":"Luxembourg has a major finance sector as well as substantial multinational activity."},{"start":171.2,"end":174.4,"text":"The same ranking can therefore hide different sources of productivity."},{"start":175.62,"end":179.2,"text":"Ireland’s result is closely connected to global company accounts."},{"start":180.38,"end":187.32,"text":"Norway’s energy resources are important, while Luxembourg’s financial services and multinational businesses play a major role."},{"start":188.62,"end":194.26,"text":"GDP per hour gives us a useful starting point, but it does not explain these differences by itself."},{"start":195.1,"end":199.08,"text":"We need to ask what kind of activity produces the measured value in each country."},{"start":199.82,"end":202.38,"text":"A careful reader should examine more than one measure."},{"start":203.82,"end":208.38,"text":"GNI per hour can be useful when multinational enterprises have a large effect on GDP."},{"start":209.58,"end":215.36,"text":"Ireland also uses modified Gross National Income, written GNI star or GNI*."},{"start":216.56,"end":221.64,"text":"It removes important globalisation effects to show a picture closer to the domestic economy."},{"start":221.64,"end":228.88,"text":"Modified GNI excludes effects connected with areas such as foreign-owned intellectual property and aircraft leasing."},{"start":229.48,"end":236.02,"text":"It is closer to income linked with workers’ pay, Irish-owned businesses, and taxes received by the government."},{"start":237.14,"end":243.06,"text":"Official results for 2025 showed that Ireland’s modified GNI was much smaller than its GDP."},{"start":243.9,"end":247.14,"text":"This does not make GDP per hour useless."},{"start":247.8,"end":253.62,"text":"Instead, it shows why several measures are needed to understand productivity and local economic life."}],"words":[{"text":"Labour","start":0,"end":0.32},{"text":"productivity","start":0.32,"end":0.92},{"text":"compares","start":0.92,"end":1.54},{"text":"economic","start":1.54,"end":2.04},{"